 3. 🐘 This week&#x27;s &quot;Trumpapalooza&quot; lineup 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Jumbotron in the GOP's midterm convention hall during a media walkthrough last month. Photo: Tony Gutierrez/AP President Trump and his top allies will share the stage with Republican candidates in some of the nation's most competitive races at this week's GOP midterm convention in Dallas, Axios' Alex Isenstadt reports from a preliminary speakers list. 

 The lineup underscores the convention's central purpose: Fire up Trump voters for November's midterms, even though the president isn't on the ballot. 🎤 Trump will speak both nights , this Wednesday and Thursday at American Airlines Center. He'll deliver the keynote on Night 1 and closing remarks on Night 2, when Vice President Vance will keynote.

 Also appearing: Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent, Health Secretary Robert F. Kennedy Jr., former White House press secretary Karoline Leavitt and Donald Trump Jr. House Speaker Mike Johnson, Senate Majority Whip John Barrasso and National Republican Senatorial Committee Chair Tim Scott are among the congressional leaders who will appear. Senate Majority Leader John Thune is notably absent from the list. He is scheduled to be in Dallas this week for fundraising, and organizers say they are still adding names to the list. 🏛️ Zoom in: The convention will spotlight Republicans in races that will determine control of Congress.

 Senate candidates include Ohio Sen. Jon Husted, Texas Republican Ken Paxton, Michigan Republican Mike Rogers, North Carolina Republican Michael Whatley and Georgia Rep. Mike Collins. Trump won all five states in 2024, and all are now competitive. Republicans in some of the toughest races aren't attending, including Sen. Susan Collins of Maine and former Sen. John Sununu of New Hampshire. On the House side, vulnerable incumbents slated to speak include New York Rep. Mike Lawler and Pennsylvania Reps. Rob Bresnahan and Ryan Mackenzie.

